[1] Every ten years, the United States Constitution requires a count of the {{1|countrys'}} population. [2] By the late 1800s, the growing nation had made this task overwhelming: clerks tallying the 1880 census by hand {{2|have needed}} nearly eight years to finish. [3] Herman Hollerith, a young engineer who had worked on that census, believed a machine could do the job faster. [4] {{3|According to one account, his idea came from watching a train conductor punch holes in tickets to record each passenger's appearance.}}

[5] In Hollerith's system, each person's information was recorded by punching holes in specific positions on a paper card. [6] When a clerk pressed a card into the machine, a set of metal pins came down on it. [7] Wherever there was a hole, a pin passed through and touched a small cup of mercury, {{4|completing}} an electrical circuit that advanced a counting dial. [8] Each dial kept a running total, and at the end of the day a clerk wrote down {{5|their}} reading.

[9] The results were dramatic. [10] {{6|For instance,}} the Census Office was able to announce the nation's total population for 1890 within weeks rather than years. [11] Hollerith's machines were soon put to work on other {{7|tasks, such as: tracking}} railroad freight and keeping business accounts. [12] In 1896, Hollerith founded the Tabulating Machine Company, which, after a merger, eventually {{8|got a new name and became}} International Business Machines, or IBM. [13] {{9|Punched cards remained a common way to store information for computers well into the 1970s.}}
